From 80b337ebcfa7cb5350da699b2d4f299f26d84222 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:40:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] HDR10+ as Dolby Vision: measure the scenes without HDR10+
 statistics

Where a picture of HDR10+ sent as Dolby Vision has no HDR10+ statistics of its
own (HDR10+ metadata from which no level 1 block can be made, or none at all),
the display was sent the last scene's level 1 block and, after twelve such
pictures, HDR10: it left Dolby Vision for credits without HDR10+ and came back
after them. The case of the credits was pointed out by the notes of a build of
CoreELEC (p3i, T4c), which keeps the last metadata there; here such scenes get
a level 1 block of their own, measured from the picture, as the user chose.

- The decoder (MakeHdr10PlusMetadata()) says what each picture is
  (dvbridge_hdr10plus_next_of(), VideoPicture::doviHdr10Plus). A picture not of
  its own statistics is no longer a repeated RPU: it is measured
  (VideoPicture::doviMeasured), with the metadata made last or, before any,
  those of its static metadata (dvbridge_measured_placeholder()). It counts as
  a picture with metadata, so the stream stays in Dolby Vision.
- The renderer measures those pictures as it measures HDR10 measured as Dolby
  Vision: the queued ones ahead of their presentation (MeasureAhead()), on the
  decoded P010 textures, only while native output is on (the only place HDR10+
  is sent as Dolby Vision). A picture of its own statistics is not measured.
- The window system (DVHdr10PlusMetadata()) asks CDVHdr10PlusFill, as each
  picture is presented, which source its scene has, and writes the measured
  level 1 block and the scene refresh into a copy of the picture's metadata
  (dvbridge_measured_apply_level1(): no level 5). A seek starts the scenes and
  the measurement again; a picture drawn again goes out as the first time.
- kodi.log, without debugging: the first measured scene of a video ("the scene
  at N s has no HDR10+ metadata / HDR10+ metadata without statistics of their
  own: its level 1 block is measured from the picture"), every 240 pictures and
  when the video ends how many scenes went out of each source, the cuts the
  measurement found, and the pictures that kept their HDR10+ scene's metadata
  or the last level 1 sent. Nothing is told for a stream whose statistics are
  all there.

A stream whose statistics are all there goes out as before, byte for byte, and
nothing of it is measured. advancedsettings.xml
<video><dvhdr10plusfill>false</dvhdr10plusfill></video> brings back the earlier
behaviour, to compare on a display.
